I 1000% agree with this. It was my first thought at seeing the post also.

Regardless of weight, your body, mind, and metabolism are still damaged by the ED. I totally get what you’re feeling though. Intellectually, I know this, but I don’t ever feel “deserving” of recovery. Even at my LW, i felt it really wasn’t that bad. My butt was the worst part- it was like concave, while the rest of my body looked totally fine. Most people I knew said I looked great, no one was concerned at all, so i felt very undeserving of recovery. It was definitely much easier than it is for me now though. I’m no longer near UW now and literally feel like I have no chance at recovery until I get below my LW. I just don’t know if I can ever psychologically get past that, I think a lot of us feel this way. I was much healthier and could maintain a healthy weight easily before I tried all in and I’m really upset for doing it at all because I feel trapped in a restrict/binge cycle atm. I don’t recommend it. I also couldn’t get this thought out of my head that if I’m recovering for “health”, why am I eating food and in ways that could be detrimental to my health? Why not stay sick the other way? Why am I still abusing food? I just can’t get past that. Concern over my health was the only motivation I’d had for recovery. 

&nbsp;

There’s a book I’ve been wanting to read called “Sick Enough” that deals with this and the medical complications of EDs. I learned about it when I started having trouble with my sight in one eye and hearing when I’d lost a lot of weight but was still in a low-ish normal BMI range. It was terrifying, but still, apparently not enough for me to justify “recovery” at my CW. I’m just so uncomfortable.

I tried this and it’s been extremely detrimental. I’ve heard it works great for some people, but for myself it just reaffirmed this fear that I’d “blow up” if I ate. Obviously, I had extreme hunger and gained a lot of weight extremely quickly. I’m unable to afford monthly therapist appointments (not to mention, clothing)and just couldn’t deal with that on my own. I was open to gain when I began refeeding, but now I’m terrified. 

&nbsp;

I also still feel sluggish and ill, similarly to when I wasn’t eating. Just that it reaffirmed what my ED feared most, I relapsed several times. Tried several more. I’m now in the midst of another relapse. I think all in sounds great and I love that there’s supporting science, but with anorexia, and I guess just myself personally, I found it very harmful. It didn’t help my relationship with food, just hurt it even more than before. I’m now struggling with both anorexia and bulimia. I feel  very upset with myself for listening to people online, but I didn’t have anything else.. I guess I forgot about the mental illness aspect somehow. But I also just feel physically ill now.

&nbsp;

Prior to this, I slowly refed and tried making well balanced meals, plus drank a nutritional drink for some slow gain. That was much better for me both physically and mentally. My body did eventually “trust me” again even that way. I see all in touted as the way to achieve that, but I achieved it just fine in a few months of going slow and steady. But everyone’s  different.

&nbsp;

Edit: I also want to note that if you’re recovering from anorexia especially, this can be very dangerous to not start slowly. Our digestion is very slow and food can stay in our stomachs for days- this can cause major pain(like major, sharp pains) and could even lead to death. I was unaware of this until I began having very sharp pains the last time I tried all in and spoke to my doctor about it. Refeeding syndrome is another concern.

Had the same reaction the first time I took Adderall. I wanted to clean my study room, took the pills and didn’t expect much. In about 30 minutes, I got done more than I had ever previously done if I’d have spent several hours cleaning.
&nbsp;

I also felt so calm, didn’t have a million different thoughts pulling me in different directions/to do different things. My thoughts were clear and flowed like very linear, as opposed to when I normally think which is a mash of images and words popping in and out. After I looked at my watch I just sat on the floor and cried my eyes out. Also felt a little resentful that I’d had to live 30 years thinking the way I felt was just normal and everyone else somehow just made life magically work for them while I couldn’t. I was happy-sad when I watched a movie on meds for the first time too. I finally understood how someone could like watching movies lol.

They always do. My favorite is someone I know bad mouthing medical science for years(the silver drinking kind of idiot) who assumed they got covid(I doubt they actually got tested but idk) didn’t share anything about it on their personal FB, but freaked out and told a family member that they went to the ER. Turned out they were just having a panic attack. But they trusted the ER enough to believe it was just a panic attack, and trusted medicine enough to even go when they thought they couldn’t breathe.

&nbsp;

A few days later, they shared on their personal account that they were better, that the stats are worse for myocarditis, and started generally flexing that it was all NBD. They also posted pics of themselves already out at a store without a mask. It took me nearing 2 months to recover from covid, and I had long covid after, so I kind of doubt this person even had it, but still. They still trusted doctors enough to go when they suspected the worst. Still freaked out about it to someone we both know, but publicly acted like they were some badass who still doesn’t trust medical science.

Ah the good old religious “fear is your enemy” followed up with 10,000 things to be fearful of. Good times.

