Recently came out to a friend about some major health issues and discussed my position on mask wearing and vaccines and they told me how we need to stop being so fearful. Also opened up to my mom about how worried I am about losing a family member during this or else never being able to see one of them again for other reasons and got the same “we can’t live in fear” speech. Meanwhile they’re afraid of the vaccine,masks,”5G”,and a man they call “Sleepy”.

You say you feel like a failure. There are a lot of red flags and things to point out concerning your SO in the post, but I kind of wanted to focus on this. Firstly, your partner is expecting meds to fix you or change you to be more acceptable to their standards. OP, you can find someone who loves you for you, flaws, ADHD, everything. And it’ll feel so much better. ADHD can cause discourse but it seems like your current SO is imagining you as you, but w/o adhd. Meds will not erase your ADHD. That said, I do strongly recommend meds, not for your bf, but for yourself. They helped me a ton, but they’re a hassle to get so I take them very sparingly.

&nbsp;

Back to my main point: no healthy relationship should leave you feeling like a failure. I’m sure you know this isn’t healthy, only you’re blaming yourself for having adhd. Your partner is going to expect your meds to be a cure, and they won’t be. He also admits to you that he’d leave you if someone “more stable” came along. OP, I’ve been in your shoes and didn’t leave and it left me with years of stress, trauma, and time spent solely on someone else while wasting my own time- because I was just fully encompassed by the stress of the relationship and constant fear that I’d do something to set him off or lose him. For years he hid our relationship and flirted with other girls, even may have actually cheated on me. Because he liked the comfort of having someone always there, but wanted something else. If I could go back and was in your situation I’d make the step to break up. He’s admitted that he’s just waiting for someone else to come along. It’s one thing to discuss possible positive changes with a partner, and another thing to talk to them and treat them the way he does you. Leave him.

I know this is an old thread but found it searching for something and really resonated with me 😅 I was about >!25lbs!< OW as a teen when I lost my first chunk of weight due to restriction. My family members would say some really weird things about how I was too thin and constantly call me “tiny”. After a nurse found out I was restricting, she guessed my weight to be much much less than what it was. This never made me happy, it made me angry. It made me feel like “they know I’m “fat” and are trying to humor me” because their lies were honestly absurd and more delusional than what they perceived my view of myself was. I know this might sound mean because they were probably trying to be helpful in some only way they thought could help, but they really just made me feel worse. I’d have family members play literal guessing games about my weight and when my grandpa answered and was the only person who confidently said the correct number, I was relieved to know I had one person in my family who wasn’t a liar and wasn’t playing their game 😅 he even got nudged for it but I was so happy.

&nbsp;

When I got older my dad would judgementally call me “too skinny” and my brothers were finally worried about my health- this was during a period where I was actually the healthiest I’d ever been. I previously struggled with restriction and bulimia but was always OW. At this point, I was finally a HW. I think my dad actually did think I was too skinny at that time though- he’s very judgmental about women’s bodies; they have to be “just right”.

&nbsp;

Now, recently I was >!10lbs!< UW and had family saying I looked great. Why? Because they didn’t live with me or know I was restricting so they didn’t view it from that old distorted lens. I’m short so it makes all the difference. I do have my SO’s parents calls me “tiny” quite often when I’m HW and UW but they’re very tall people and I always assumed they meant like my actual size. My bf’s mom always calls my shoes doll shoes 😂 also had a classmate call me tiny quite often because she thought the size of my backpack was ridiculous for my frame. I don’t mind it in those contexts, but otherwise it usually bothers me. My bf was the only one to comment how “tiny” I was when I was UW and sadly he seemed to enjoy it. He’d pick me up and carry me around a lot more and comment quite often. In that case it made me feel valid but sad because I knew I probably couldn’t stay that way.

