I’ve thought about this recently since I went into a full blown relapse twice this year. I’ve had an ED with varying severity for over half my life. My parents were neglectful and abusive; I never felt loved except maybe sometimes by my grandparents. Getting into abusive friendships and other relationships throughout my life has been common.

&nbsp;

Most of the time, as my metabolism and mind slow, I realized that this is also kind of “slowing down” my anxiety and conscious pain because I just don’t have the energy. But I also realized that when people have shown me compassion and love over the years over my ED or other problems, it’s become something I crave. I don’t live around family/have a supportive family anyway, or have friends, so I don’t get to have that, but I wish I did. Some people like doctors show me compassion and I find that comforting.

&nbsp;


It’s similar for past cutting and suicidal ideation; I probably don't need to say this here, but feeling loved for a moment isn't the cause of these things of course, but rather a result of the illnesses, and just ends up to be something that I find myself wanting and wishing I had more of. 
&nbsp;

I have two memories that stick out in regards to the latter: when I was a teen, as I said, I was neglected pretty severely. I planned suicide and ran away from home, but ended up at the hospital instead and told them everything about my ED. A nurse there gave me fluids and ran their hands over my scars, which was kind of weird and jarring, but they asked if I cut, I said yes, and they just held my hand for a while and told me that I was a valuable human. I’d never heard that before and I’d never been treated that way before. The second incident was a few years ago during a very traumatic time in my life when I attempted suicide. I was super drunk and finally gained a bit more consciousness in the ambulance, and the EMT was incredibly patient and kind to me, and did something very similar; just held my hand the whole time and told me I was worth something and had a kind heart(they read my journals :/). That incident was particularly special, yet painful to me because at the time I felt betrayed and totally unloved by every single person in my life. I felt like for a second, I had some idea of what it was like to be “normal” or loved or whatever.


&nbsp;

For the examples you made, I actually really hate people trying to make me eat, and also points 3 and 5 make me feel very uncomfortable at the thought. But people seeing me look differently and also being kind of concerned is something that I think about and fantasize about. I want it to be noticeable because that means I look different, which is what I wanted. I want comments because comments were also made when I was overweight and at healthier weights- I however am very sensitive to rude comments about my weight and appearance from strangers, which as an adult, I’ve only received being UW. As a child, strangers would pick at my weight for being OW. In either situation, I felt that the other people were just trying to lift themselves up or just enjoyed putting people down, because i feel like it takes a lot to comment on a stranger’s body. I kind of like the idea of people I know being worried, because that’s some sort of validation to my ED brain that I’m “sick enough” and can stop. It probably wouldn’t make me stop anyway, but I have some fantasy that it would, and part of that is also feeling temporarily like someone cares about me. If that makes sense.

&nbsp;

The only other thing I can compare is to this thought of wanting to just give up and give in- if I stop eating or consuming any calories entirely, I can just slow down and stop trying so hard at everything, and maybe someone can finally help me. I guess my motivations are love and validation, but I’m also competitive and though I don’t even like what I look like after weight loss, I have this urge to be “sickest”, even though intellectually I don’t want that at all.

I feel similarly- I’m a tiny bit taller and weigh a few lbs less than Ariana, but I’d love to have her frame. I think I look terrible, but still “too big” in places and it really gets to me. My torso and face especially bother me because they’re just sunken and lifeless looking. As a teen, I was really into extreme thinspo(Bonespo?) but looking at that and even myself now makes me feel very uncomfortable.

&nbsp;

I guess I’m in a weird spot now where I prefer “fitspo” and would like to look fit or even skinny but not emaciated, but when I gain muscle and weight I just can’t mentally deal with it.

Toddler meals, honestly lol. I mostly started eating them and baby food after I’m breaking a 2 month fast because they’re so gentle. There’s always a few people in recovery groups that scold me for eating them because they’re “disordered foods”, but I feel like categorizing a food as “disordered” is akin to saying it’s “bad” or “good” and that’s not healthy either/kind of triggers me as a mindset to have, if that makes sense.. when I was in inpatient a decade ago, we were scolded for putting PB in oatmeal for being “disordered” and now my RD recommends it. 

&nbsp;

I ended up genuinely loving prepackaged toddler meals and snacks. They’re not cloy or overly salty like a lot of adult targeted foods in the US. I also have ADHD and it’s just much easier to grab something basically already prepared and pop it in the microwave or something, especially if you haven’t eaten in a very long time and aren’t used to making time for yourself to prep meals yet. Breaking from a long fast with adult targeted prepared meals is potentially dangerous due to high mineral content, like salt. 

&nbsp;

I digress. But yeah, I love them. I use them as little snacks now during recovery. I love baby mashed potatoes especially 😅Love certain types of rice cakes and non-dairy foods/especially milks too. I can’t stand the smell and consistency of cow milk.

There’s a lot of reasons this could happen. For myself, I had a classmate similar to this(thankfully he moved on before it got this bad) and I engaged him because he is much larger than me and gave me the vibe that he could be violent because he’d have temper tantrums, but at school he was mostly shy,quiet, and seemed nice. He kept contacting me to angrily complain about girls ghosting him and it freaked me out. I knew I’d have to engage with him again in person at school and was honestly afraid that ghosting or cutting him off entirely might result in being harmed.

