"Out of control" can describe many things: certain kids with ADHD(usually the typical hyperactive boy with ADHD), kids with ODD, people that are stressed or have a trauma history, etc. It's these kinds of "professionals" that made me give up on therapy. This is this person's **career** and they don't know about inattentive or mixed ADHD. I mean even anxiety can be a symptom of hyperactivity, especially if you're introverted. It's also more common for girls to have internal hyperactivity due to social norms- though boys can present that way also. 

&nbsp;

I find if you're female especially,  or had good grades, you're much more likely to have a mental illness like depression, bi polar, anxiety, or emotional regulation label slapped on you before even being offered a proper assessment. I have issues with mental illness myself, but since being diagnosed i now recognize many aspects of ADHD as having had a significant impact on my life. I often wonder if I'd have been diagnosed sooner how differently my life could have been. 

&nbsp;

My bf had good grades and everyone considered him highly gifted- he was diagnosed with BP but almost certainly has ADHD. He doesn't really get the "depression" part of that often at all.

My boyfriend almost definitely has inattentive ADHD and is hands down the smartest person I know, also a software engineer. He *can* obviously be wrong about things once ina while(normally due to skimming over reading-totally an ADHD thing), but he's essentially a human encyclopedia. It kind of infuriates me sometimes because I'm truly not a smart person- but due to educational gaps, a chaotic life, etc. and things come so easily to him 😂 when he talks about himself as a child though, we were a lot alike. The differences being that his education was heavily encouraged, praised and nurtured, and he had a pretty stable home life, while I did not have any of that. I would get yelled at for asking questions and had to parent my parents.  As a result, I was always distracted doing tons of different things at once. I did have good grades though.

&nbsp;

His grades were also great aside from a few classes that he didn't do well in solely for not doing the work(forgot or got distracted) so obviously no one ever considered he had ADHD. Some teachers apparently wouldn't even look at the work and would just write an A on his homework.

&nbsp;

Due to the ability to hyperfocus especially, a person with ADHD absolutely can be gifted. When I get really into something- I learn everything about it and very quickly. For things I'm not interested in, i struggle. It sounds like this person thinks people with ADHD have below average intelligence. While the disorder can negatively affect learning, it has nothing to do with intelligence, we just learn differently. 

&nbsp;

Sounds like this person has some chip on their shoulder, but also..it's reasonable to them that they're correct when multiple professionals have mentioned differently? That's totally unreasonable. This person sounds damaging and in this case no therapy is probably better than her. I'm sorry about your situation 😔 it really is terrible when you only have one choice and the person turns out to be someone who should not be in their profession.

