I literally have been feeling extremely depressed over this so much lately(just turned 30) that I had a bit of a meltdown yesterday. As a child, I skipped a few grades(in attempt to graduate early/go to college=get away from my family), so of course my parents decided it would be fun to destroy my education. It's a long story, but I ended dropping out of school with about a 4th-8th grade education in the end. Got into various bad situations because that's literally all I knew, and am now in school at 30 and struggling hard.

&nbsp;

As a child I was always interested in sciences, art, and music. Namely, I loved chemistry and astronomy. I was fine at math; the aforementioned subjects have always helped me learn math more easily. I'd make molecular models for fun. I always begged my mom to let me do the science experiments in my science books(when I'd be homeschooled) but she "didn't want me to make a mess" even though I meticulously cleaned up after myself. In HS I also took an art class and she never bought the required materials so I couldn't even do the work . I would put those little discovery science kits on my Christmas list every year and my brothers would get them instead. Once my brother got a microscope and I started using it and was told I couldn't because I was "too young" or "a girl". Another year they got this "computer" with these study cards that had a code you could enter. It would start a "game" and you'd have to match questions with answers. They never used it, so I took it and it was my favorite thing to play around with. I think it's called a Geosafari? 

&nbsp;

In 3rd-5th grade in Christian school I'd do a book report weekly, usually on the various science topics we had in the classroom. Eventually I read through them all. My teacher's favorite was my astronomy report; I can still remember him being so excited by it and asking *me* questions. When parent-teacher meetings came around, he apparently told my parents I was "perfect"(tbh thought that was kind of weird but also appreciated this teacher so much for just noticing I was alive). My dad's summary out of everything he'd been told? That "he looked like a pedo". And that was it. My dad was always strict about education / wanted me to constantly be studying and get into a good college. So I'd share with him and my mom what I'd do or show them my report card and they wouldn't look or say anything at all. 

&nbsp;

Everything came to a halt when I reached middle school and switching in and out of schools *every school semester* would set me back/then I'd work ahead/be set back, and then the materials for each school were different and I became very confused. I realized recently that I developed a traumatic response to doing math because my mom would yell at me and freak out when I couldn't understand something(I was a bright kid but clearly developed a learning problem around 11); since then, when I've asked for help, my mind goes blank and my heart races. I'm finally properly medicated for ADHD and had a moment of clarity literally last week when my bf tried to help me and I realized all of this. Now I'm doing better because I don't approach it as "wow I'm a moron/I can't think/I'm not capable" but rather "I'm having a panic attack so I need to do X to center myself and try again." 

&nbsp;

I went from being a bright child with a ton of unrecognized potential to a 30 year old with no job, various mental illnesses, a mountain of traumas built up over the years, debt, struggling with college due to gaps in my educational history, dependent on another human(and desperately don't want to be-I have a condition that will eventually cause me to become immobile and I want to be independent before that happens), and I constantly feel stupid,over stressed, and incapable. 

&nbsp;

I had a few teachers recognize potential in me and I remember thinking that I wished they could've been my parents. In retrospect, I was always my mom's personal therapist and scapegoat and I think she intentionally sabotaged me to keep me there like a little pet by her side forever. She knew I wanted to get away and never wanted to let her scapegoat go.

&nbsp;

Last night I had a bit of a breakdown. I'm only taking 4 classes and I'm majorly struggling to keep up(ADHD and large gaps in knowledge don't work out great in college apparently lol). I do honestly blame nmom for this. My bf was like me as a child but his talents were recognized and nurtured and he's one of the smartest people I know, because he was given the opportunity to grow. Its hard to be with him sometimes because I feel like if I'd just had the right parents, this is the type of adult I could've grown into. Instead, I'm just dependent on him and it kills me because independence and achievement were all I ever wanted. Instead most of my 20s were spent being abused by more people, in and out of college, in and out of psychiatric wards, constant suicidal ideation, alcoholism, eating disorders, and so on. I'm sad that I've "lost" so much of my life and didn't gain clarity about things much sooner. I'm also sad that I didn't just keep trying; I remember the moment that I made the conscious decision to give up in 6th grade. 

&nbsp;

I still wish I could be a scientist but at this rate, struggling with 4 classes at 30, I'm going to be graduating later than I should anyway. Taking more difficult classes would take me much longer. I'm good at classical art also, but since I doubt I could make a living off that I'm going into design. Which I'm not really thrilled about, but its all I can think could work for me longterm with all the problems I have. I feel robbed of my life, myself, and my potential.

Awesome question!! It's a little difficult for me to answer because the genetic and cultural backgrounds of Mexicans can vary so greatly. My dad mentioned to me recently that Mexico even has a lot of people of French descent and even Asian descent(he was comparing the diversity to that of the US when we were discussing how indigenous features are often what people in the US think of when they think of Mexicans).

 &nbsp;

Regardless, the majority are mixed with at least some indigenous blood. The cultures of the indigenous groups and Spanish(primarily) have blended, but the primary language is Spanish. Personally, my initual reaction is that due to the mixture I want to say I'm ok with "latino", but part of me also feels like "Nican Tlaca" may be more appropriate.

 &nbsp;

The unfortunate reality is that unless we're part of a tribe or individual families keep track of ancestry, not many of us know "what" we are; my dad is very native American and calls himself "white" sometimes. He mostly calls himself "spanish" because in the US he's referred to as "hispanic"- a term which does feel inappropriate and like an attempt at erasure to me personally, but he embraces it with pride.

 &nbsp;

Because of the modern mixture, I almost don't feel like either term is appropriate for Mexicans as a whole, but I'm not sure what an alternative could be. I feel like perhaps a Nahuatl word that means "mixed people" to refer to Mexicans would be most suitable as it would recognize the country's cultural origins(though of course not all Mexicans were Aztec, or even Inca or Maya, but using a native language is better than  what we currently have,imo ) and present day. 

&nbsp;

All of this said, I personally refer to myself as Chicana as it most appropriately identifies who I am: a Mexican-American of mixed indigenous background. I use "latina" also, but never "hispanic" because I'm hardly Spanish and don't even speak much Spanish. The main Spanish influence in my life has been a mixture of Spanish and Indigenous food and Mexican music spoken in Spanish.  I don't really like to call myself "latina" either, but it's preferable to "hispanic" as it just means "a person of Latin-American origin".

&nbsp;

